WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:03.501
-Welcome back to the show.
I always love having you on.

00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:04.701
-Thanks, honey.

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:05.901
-Last time you were on,
it was right before

00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:08.033
you won the Oscar for
"The Eyes of Tammy Faye."

00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:10.367
-Can you believe it?!
-Yes!

00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:11.934
-What?!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.934 --> 00:00:13.200
-Touchdown!
-You won the Oscar!

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.067
I mean, touchdown!
Oh, my gosh!

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:17.767
You actually posted this.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:19.000
"Did this actually happen
two weeks ago?

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.567
Asking for a friend."

00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:22.601
With you holding --
I mean, congratulations.

00:00:22.601 --> 00:00:24.801
-Thanks, man.
-You were unbelievable in that.

00:00:24.801 --> 00:00:26.300
And I called it
when we were talking.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.534
I was like, "Oh, my gosh.
It was so amazing."

00:00:27.534 --> 00:00:29.868
So I think I --
-I was shocked. I really was.

00:00:29.868 --> 00:00:31.133
-Really?

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:34.434
-Yeah, I was completely not
expecting it at all.

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:37.467
-The more people told me it
was going to happen, I was like,

00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:40.534
"It's not, but thank you
for being so nice to me."

00:00:40.534 --> 00:00:42.834
-I mean, it's just
when you get that hyped up,

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:44.267
but you're down to like,
you know,

00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:45.634
whatever -- however
many nominees are in there,

00:00:45.634 --> 00:00:47.000
it's like you just get nervous.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.767
You're like, "One of us
is going to go home with it."

00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:49.901
But you crushed it.

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:51.667
It was so good
and well-deserved.

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:54.167
Congrats on that.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:56.100
I was so happy for you.

00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:00.100
And now, recently, you made this
announcement yesterday.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.000
You're going back to Broadway.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.000
-I am.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.133
I know.
-Are you excited?

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:06.434
-I'm so excited.

00:01:06.434 --> 00:01:08.434
I'm going to play Nora in
"A Doll's House."

00:01:08.434 --> 00:01:10.234
-Wow!
-Jamie Lloyd is directing.

00:01:10.234 --> 00:01:11.701
Amy Herzog did the adaptation.

00:01:11.701 --> 00:01:15.701
And, yeah, it's, you know,
just an iconic theater role,

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:17.367
so no pressure.

00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:19.434
-Yeah. You're like,
"Why do I do this to myself?"

00:01:19.434 --> 00:01:21.801
-Yeah.
-Do you like doing live theater?

00:01:21.801 --> 00:01:23.834
-Oh, yes and no.

00:01:23.834 --> 00:01:26.400
I mean, the last time I did it
was like a decade ago.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.367
The great thing about live
theater is anything can happen.

00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:32.534
And I think that's part of the
feeling with the audience, too.

00:01:32.534 --> 00:01:35.534
When I go to live theater,
I'm like, "Ooh, there's a pause.

00:01:35.534 --> 00:01:36.567
Did someone forget their lines?"

00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:38.634
It's, like, this exciting thing.
-Yeah.

00:01:38.634 --> 00:01:42.167
-And the last time I did it,
it was just me and my dog.

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:45.000
And my dog, Chaplin,
has three legs.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.634
So when he walks, he, like,

00:01:46.634 --> 00:01:48.868
makes this hopping sound
because of his tags.

00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:51.033
It's like,
"Jing-a-jing-a-jing-a-jing."

00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:52.567
-Yeah.

00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:55.100
-And I was onstage, performing,
and there's a note

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.801
on my dressing-room door,
"Please don't open the door."

00:01:56.801 --> 00:01:58.467
-Oh, no.
-And it's a very serious scene.

00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:02.033
And, all of a sudden, I hear,
"Jing-a-jing-a-jing-a-jing."

00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:04.667
And I'm on the stage --
Like, David Strathairn and I

00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:06.234
are having
a very dramatic moment.

00:02:06.234 --> 00:02:07.400
And then it's,
"Jing-a-jing-a-jing-a-jing."

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.834
And then it stops.

00:02:08.834 --> 00:02:10.400
And the audience kind of goes...
[ Gasps ]

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.767
And I thought, "Yep, he's right
next to me right now."

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:17.234
So I just -- I looked at David
and I started saying a line

00:02:17.234 --> 00:02:18.601
and then I walked offstage.

00:02:18.601 --> 00:02:20.901
And then Chaplin was like,
"Jing-a-jing-a-jing-a-jing."

00:02:20.901 --> 00:02:22.100
Following me.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.634
-Made his debut, Broadway debut.
-Picked him up.

00:02:23.634 --> 00:02:25.067
I took him back out
for curtain call.

00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:26.133
-Oh, did you really?
-Yeah.

00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:28.300
-Yeah, that's a good mom.
Come on, yeah.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.133
Are you going to bring --
-Oh, no.

00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:32.434
-No.
-His rights have been revoked.

00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:33.767
Yeah.

00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:36.501
-But this is gonna be
exciting -- back on Broadway.

00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:37.701
-I'm excited.

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:39.734
-I also wanted to ask you
a couple more things.

00:02:39.734 --> 00:02:41.601
Recently,
I saw you brought up --

00:02:41.601 --> 00:02:43.434
You were doing an interview
and you said

00:02:43.434 --> 00:02:45.334
you and Anne Hathaway
thought of remaking

00:02:45.334 --> 00:02:47.601
"Death Becomes Her,"
which originally starred

00:02:47.601 --> 00:02:48.801
Meryl Streep, Goldie Hawn,

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.934
And I go, "Ooh,
that's kind of a good idea."

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:53.767
-Yeah, well, this interview
was many months ago,

00:02:53.767 --> 00:02:55.000
and I was shooting with Annie.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.400
And the interviewer said,

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.701
"You know, is there a remake
that you would like to do?"

00:02:58.701 --> 00:03:00.234
And I was like,
"Well, I don't know."

00:03:00.234 --> 00:03:03.133
And, well, funny enough,
Annie and I were on set,

00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:05.667
and we said, "That would be
a great movie to do.

00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:07.067
That would be so much fun."

00:03:07.067 --> 00:03:09.033
And I said this
to the interviewer,

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:11.000
and then, of course, all of a
sudden, it's everywhere.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.067
"They're going to remake
'Death Becomes Her.'"

00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:13.467
-Yeah.

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:15.100
-But, I mean, I don't think
you can remake that movie --

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.868
unless you swap the genders.

00:03:17.868 --> 00:03:21.067
So it's two men drinking
the magic potion

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:23.434
to try to, like,
stay young and sexy forever,

00:03:23.434 --> 00:03:25.567
and they're fighting over
the love of a woman.

00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:27.601
-That's the take. Yes.
There you go.

00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:29.067
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah.

00:03:29.067 --> 00:03:30.400
-I love that.
-Yeah.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.367
-Alright, so, it might --
-Send in your tape.

00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:35.767
-I'm going to send in my tape.
You're going to love my acting.

00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:37.400
It's so good.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.067
There was also a rumor
I heard that

00:03:39.067 --> 00:03:41.467
you would like to play
a Bond villain.

00:03:41.467 --> 00:03:46.601
-Well, yeah, when I started in
the industry about 10 years ago,

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:49.167
everyone kept asking me, "Would
you like to be a Bond Girl?"

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:51.934
Because it was -- For a woman
in the industry, it was like,

00:03:51.934 --> 00:03:54.667
ooh, the pinnacle of success
to play a Bond Girl.

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:56.968
And for me, I was like,
"No, I want to be the villain."

00:03:56.968 --> 00:03:59.767
Like, I love this idea of, like,
Bond being afraid of me.

00:03:59.767 --> 00:04:02.100
That, like -- I love that.
-Yeah.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.467
-And, so, yeah, of course,
anyone wants to call me

00:04:04.467 --> 00:04:06.033
with a Bond-villain role,
I'm there.

00:04:06.033 --> 00:04:08.701
-Yeah. They're actually looking
for a new James Bond.

00:04:08.701 --> 00:04:10.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:12.501
I mean, talking about
switching the roles.

00:04:12.501 --> 00:04:14.534
You could be the
first female James Bond.

00:04:14.534 --> 00:04:16.667
-No. No interest at all.
-What?!

00:04:16.667 --> 00:04:17.868
-No, no.
-Why not?

00:04:17.868 --> 00:04:19.367
-I might be the only person
who's ever said

00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:20.901
they have no interest in
playing James Bond.

00:04:20.901 --> 00:04:21.901
-You don't want the...

00:04:21.901 --> 00:04:22.901
[ Vocalizes
"The James Bond" theme ]

00:04:22.901 --> 00:04:24.100
-No. No interest.
-What?!

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.000
-I want to torture James Bond!

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.267
-Okay. Alright, you can
torture James Bond.

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:31.667
-Let me have my dream, Jimmy!

00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:34.667
-Yes, I don't want to get in
the way of your dreams ever.

00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:36.200
I want to talk about
"George &amp; Tammy,"

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.534
because I believe the last time
I interviewed you,

00:04:38.534 --> 00:04:40.701
you were in your makeup chair
doing this.

00:04:40.701 --> 00:04:42.267
-Yes, we were on Zoom.
-Yes.

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:44.801
I appreciate it you doing that.
-Of course.

00:04:44.801 --> 00:04:48.133
George Jones, Tammy Wynette --
two legends,

00:04:48.133 --> 00:04:49.300
country-music legends.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.934
-I know. Again, no pressure.
-Yeah, I know.

00:04:50.934 --> 00:04:54.067
Why do you do this to yourself?
-Because I'm a masochist.

00:04:54.067 --> 00:04:57.067
-But, I mean, how do you get
involved in this role?

00:04:57.067 --> 00:04:58.868
Do you -- I know you sing,

00:04:58.868 --> 00:05:00.400
but do you get intimidated
when you --

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.634
-I mean, I like to say I can
tell a story through song.

00:05:03.634 --> 00:05:06.267
I never really thought of myself
as a singer.

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:08.634
I sing in "Tammy Faye,"
but that's also, like,

00:05:08.634 --> 00:05:10.234
singing up to Jesus,
and so there was, like,

00:05:10.234 --> 00:05:13.267
a sense of camp to it that --
Tammy Wynette hasn't --

00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:14.400
I mean, she's serious.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.467
You know, it's very serious
when she --

00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:16.767
-But you sound fantastic.

00:05:16.767 --> 00:05:18.834
-Oh, geez.
Thanks for saying that.

00:05:18.834 --> 00:05:20.400
-I'm sorry to make you --
I'm sorry.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.667
I don't want to embarrass you.
I'm sorry.

00:05:21.667 --> 00:05:22.934
But I have to talk about this.

00:05:22.934 --> 00:05:24.534
-I mean, all I really knew
about her was

00:05:24.534 --> 00:05:27.701
she sang the song
"Stand By Your Man."

00:05:27.701 --> 00:05:31.033
But, you know, there was a lot
of controversy about that song.

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:32.868
And, you know,
she kept saying that song

00:05:32.868 --> 00:05:33.934
doesn't mean women
are a doormat.

00:05:33.934 --> 00:05:36.868
And, in reality,
she married five times.

00:05:36.868 --> 00:05:40.567
So the song should have been
like "Stand By Your Men."

00:05:40.567 --> 00:05:41.701
-She had a lot, yeah.

00:05:41.701 --> 00:05:44.000
Here's pic of you and
Michael Shannon as

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.067
George and Tammy.

00:05:45.067 --> 00:05:47.801
How is Michael Shannon?
-Oh, great.

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:49.334
-He's a friend of the show.
-I know.

00:05:49.334 --> 00:05:52.167
I think I've seen him in a cape
on this show or something once.

00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:54.300
-Yeah, he was dressed as, like,
Alvin and the Chipmunks once.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.200
He just came out -- He's just a
fun, fun human being.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:58.434
I love that guy.
-I know.

00:05:58.434 --> 00:06:01.133
We worked together 11 years ago
on a movie called

00:06:01.133 --> 00:06:02.167
"Take Shelter."

00:06:02.167 --> 00:06:03.267
-Oh, yeah.

00:06:03.267 --> 00:06:04.534
-And so we've been
friends since.

00:06:04.534 --> 00:06:06.434
And he's a musician.

00:06:06.434 --> 00:06:09.501
I'd gone to see his band,
Corporal, play.

00:06:09.501 --> 00:06:10.567
And so I knew
he would be great for this.

00:06:10.567 --> 00:06:12.167
-Yeah, and he can sing. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:12.167 --> 00:06:14.033
-It's fantastic.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:14.033 --> 00:06:16.767
Here's Jessica Chastain and
Michael Shannon in

00:06:16.767 --> 00:06:17.834
"George &amp; Tammy."

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:19.200
Take a look at this.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:22.267
-Our first time.

00:06:22.267 --> 00:06:24.567
[ Click ]

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:27.067
♪♪

00:06:27.067 --> 00:06:30.234
-♪ Take me ♪

00:06:30.234 --> 00:06:34.167
♪ Take me to your darkest room ♪

00:06:34.167 --> 00:06:41.934
♪ Close every window
and bolt every door ♪

00:06:41.934 --> 00:06:45.267
♪ The very first moment ♪

00:06:45.267 --> 00:06:49.167
♪ I heard your voice ♪

00:06:49.167 --> 00:06:54.200
♪ I'd be in darkness no more ♪

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:58.701
-Oh, my gosh! Come on!
Jessica Chastain!

00:06:58.701 --> 00:07:00.100
What more do you want?

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:01.901
"George &amp; Tammy" premieres

00:07:01.901 --> 00:07:04.734
December 4th at 9:00 p.m.
on Showtime.

00:07:04.734 --> 00:07:06.734
We'll be right back with
Martha Stewart.

00:07:06.734 --> 00:07:07.801
Stick around, everybody.

